    {%- when 'opal' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--opal">
        <h2>Care & Treatment</h2>
        <p>Opal is softer than many gemstones and responds best to gentleness. It is not fragile when handled with awareness — but it does not forgive harshness.</p>
        <p>Do:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Clean gently with a soft cloth after wear</li>
          <li>Use mild soap and lukewarm water occasionally — for Australian Opals only</li>
          <li>Store separately in a padded box away from harder stones</li>
          <li>Ensure the setting is secure, especially in rings</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Water contact for Ethiopian Opals — can cause temporary cloudiness</li>
          <li>Perfumes, lotions, detergents, and alcohol-based products</li>
          <li>Ultrasonic or steam cleaning</li>
          <li>Heat and sudden temperature changes</li>
          <li>Gym sessions, manual work, or any physical impact while wearing</li>
        </ul>
        <p>On treatments: All our Opals are 100% natural, untreated, and uncoated. What is never acceptable for astrological use — doublets, triplets, wax filling, resin filling, or any composite stone sold as solid. If a stone is not confirmed as solid, natural, untreated, and certified, it should not be purchased for astrological purposes.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'ruby' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--ruby">
        <h2>Care & Treatment</h2>
        <p>Ruby is one of the hardest gemstones in the world — <strong>Mohs 9</strong> — and is well suited to daily wear when properly set. It asks only for basic mindfulness, not delicate handling.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Do:</strong> Clean with a soft, lint-free cloth after wear. Use lukewarm water and a drop of mild soap occasionally. Store separately in a padded box. Ensure the setting is secure and check prongs or bezels regularly, particularly in rings worn daily.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid:</strong> Ultrasonic or steam cleaning. Harsh chemicals, acids, perfumes, and detergents. Extreme heat and sudden temperature changes. Remove during gym sessions, manual work, or contact sports. Keep away from diamonds and other harder stones in storage.</li>
          <li><strong>On treatments:</strong> Untreated Rubies are rare, the most valuable, and strongly preferred for astrological use. Heat-treated stones are stable and acceptable when fully disclosed. What is never acceptable — and what we never carry — are glass-filled, lead-filled, or diffusion-treated stones. These are structurally compromised, unsuitable for astrological wear, and a significant misrepresentation of value. Colour fading over time is a direct red flag for poor or unstable treatment.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
